## I GENERAL

## Interpretation and application

## 44.1

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (1) In Parts 44 to 47, unless the context otherwise requires –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘authorised court officer’ means any officer of –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (i) the County Court;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (ii) a district registry;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (iii) the Family Court;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (iiia) the High Court; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (iv) the Costs Office,

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] whom the Lord Chancellor has authorised to assess costs;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘conditional fee agreement’ means an agreement enforceable under section 58 of the Courts and Legal Services Act 1990 1 ;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘costs’ includes fees, charges, disbursements, expenses, remuneration, reimbursement allowed to a litigant in person under rule 46.5 and any fee or reward charged by a lay representative for acting on behalf of a party in proceedings allocated to the small claims track;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘costs judge’ means a taxing master of the Senior Courts;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘Costs Office’ means the Senior Courts Costs Office;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘costs officer’ means –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (i) a costs judge;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (ii) a District Judge; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (iii) an authorised court officer;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘detailed assessment’ means the procedure by which the amount of costs is decided by a costs officer in accordance with Part 47;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘the Director (legal aid)’ means the person designated as the Director of Legal Aid Casework pursuant to section 4 of the Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012 2 , or a person entitled to exercise the functions of the Director;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘fixed costs’ means costs, the amounts of which are fixed by these Rules;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘free of charge’ has the same meaning as in section 194(10) of the 2007 Act;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘fund’ includes any estate or property held for the benefit of any person or class of person and any fund to which a trustee or personal representative is entitled in that capacity;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘HMRC’ means HM Revenue and Customs;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘legal aid’ means civil legal services made available under arrangements made for the purposes of Part 1of the Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘paying party’ means a party liable to pay costs;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘the prescribed charity’ has the same meaning as in section 194(8) of the 2007 Act;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘pro bono representation’ means legal representation provided free of charge;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘receiving party’ means a party entitled to be paid costs;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘summary assessment’ means the procedure whereby costs are assessed by the judge who has decided the case or application or where rule 44.6(2) applies;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘VAT’ means Value Added Tax;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] ‘the 2007 Act’ means the Legal Services Act 2007 3 . [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (‘Legal representative’ has the meaning given in rule 2.3). [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (2) The costs to which Parts 44 to 47 apply include –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (a) the following costs where those costs may be assessed by the court –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (i) costs of proceedings before an arbitrator or umpire;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (ii) costs of proceedings before a tribunal or other statutory body; and

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (iii) costs payable by a client to their legal representative; and

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (b) costs which are payable by one party to another party under the terms of a contract, where the court makes an order for an assessment of those costs. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.1] (3) Where advocacy or litigation services are provided to a client under a conditional fee agreement, costs are recoverable under Parts 44 to 47 notwithstanding that the client is liable to pay the legal representative’s fees and expenses only to the extent that sums are recovered in respect of the proceedings, whether by way of costs or otherwise. ## Court’s discretion as to costs

## 44.2

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (1) The court has discretion as to –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (a) whether costs are payable by one party to another;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (b) the amount of those costs; and

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (c) when they are to be paid. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (2) If the court decides to make an order about costs –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (a) the general rule is that the unsuccessful party will be ordered to pay the costs of the successful party; but

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (b) the court may make a different order. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (3) The general rule does not apply to the following proceedings –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (a) proceedings in the Court of Appeal on an application or appeal made in connection with proceedings in the Family Division; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (b) proceedings in the Court of Appeal from a judgment, direction, decision or order given or made in probate proceedings or family proceedings. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (4) In deciding what order (if any) to make about costs, the court will have regard to all the circumstances, including –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (a) the conduct of all the parties;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (b) whether a party has succeeded on part of its case, even if that party has not been wholly successful; and

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (c) any admissible offer to settle made by a party which is drawn to the court’s attention, and which is not an offer to which costs consequences under Part 36 apply. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (5) The conduct of the parties includes –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (a) conduct before, as well as during, the proceedings and in particular the extent to which the parties followed the Practice Direction – Pre-Action Conduct or any relevant pre-action protocol;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (b) whether it was reasonable for a party to raise, pursue or contest a particular allegation or issue;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (c) the manner in which a party has pursued or defended its case or a particular allegation or issue;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (d) whether a claimant who has succeeded in the claim, in whole or in part, exaggerated its claim; and

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (e)whether a party failed to comply with an order for alternative dispute resolution, or unreasonably failed to engage in alternative dispute resolution. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (6) The orders which the court may make under this rule include an order that a party must pay –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (a) a proportion of another party’s costs;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (b) a stated amount in respect of another party’s costs;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (c) costs from or until a certain date only;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (d) costs incurred before proceedings have begun;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (e) costs relating to particular steps taken in the proceedings;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (f) costs relating only to a distinct part of the proceedings; and

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (g) interest on costs from or until a certain date, including a date before judgment. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (7) Before the court considers making an order under paragraph (6)(f), it will consider whether it is practicable to make an order under paragraph (6)(a) or (c) instead. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (8) Where the court orders a party to pay costs subject to detailed assessment, it will order that party to pay a reasonable sum on account of costs, unless there is good reason not to do so. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (9) In respect of a SLAPP claim, a court may not exercise its discretion so as to order a defendant to pay a claimant’s costs except where, in the court’s view, misconduct of the defendant in relation to the claim justifies such an order. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (10) For the purposes of paragraph (9), a SLAPP claim is strategic litigation against public participation being a SLAPP claim within the meaning of section 195 of the Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act 2023. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.2] (Rules 20.2 and 20.3 provide that counterclaims and other additional claims are treated as claims and that references to a claimant or a defendant include a party bringing or defending an additional claim.)

## Basis of assessment

## 44.3

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (1) Where the court is to assess the amount of costs (whether by summary or detailed assessment) it will assess those costs –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (a) on the standard basis; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (b) on the indemnity basis,

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] but the court will not in either case allow costs which have been unreasonably incurred or are unreasonable in amount. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (Rule 44.5 sets out how the court decides the amount of costs payable under a contract.)

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (2) Where the amount of costs is to be assessed on the standard basis, the court will –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (a) only allow costs which are proportionate to the matters in issue. Costs which are disproportionate in amount may be disallowed or reduced even if they were reasonably or necessarily incurred; and

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (b) resolve any doubt which it may have as to whether costs were reasonably and proportionately incurred or were reasonable and proportionate in amount in favour of the paying party. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (Factors which the court may take into account are set out in rule 44.4.)

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (3) Where the amount of costs is to be assessed on the indemnity basis, the court will resolve any doubt which it may have as to whether costs were reasonably incurred or were reasonable in amount in favour of the receiving party. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (4) Where –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (a) the court makes an order about costs without indicating the basis on which the costs are to be assessed; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (b) the court makes an order for costs to be assessed on a basis other than the standard basis or the indemnity basis,

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] the costs will be assessed on the standard basis. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (5) Costs incurred are proportionate if they bear a reasonable relationship to –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (a) the sums in issue in the proceedings;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (b) the value of any non-monetary relief in issue in the proceedings;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (c) the complexity of the litigation;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (d) any additional work generated by the conduct of the paying party,

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (e) any wider factors involved in the proceedings, such as reputation or public importance; and

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (f) any additional work undertaken or expense incurred due to the vulnerability of a party or any witness. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (6) Where the amount of a solicitor’s remuneration in respect of non-contentious business is regulated by any general orders made under the Solicitors Act 1974 4 , the amount of the costs to be allowed in respect of any such business which falls to be assessed by the court will be decided in accordance with those general orders rather than this rule and rule 44.4. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (7) Paragraphs (2)(a) and (5) do not apply in relation to –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (a) cases commenced before 1st April 2013; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] (b) costs incurred in respect of work done before 1st April 2013,

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.3] and in relation to such cases or costs, rule 44.4.(2)(a) as it was in force immediately before 1st April 2013 will apply instead. ## Factors to be taken into account in deciding the amount of costs

## 44.4

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (1) The court will have regard to all the circumstances in deciding whether costs were –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (a) if it is assessing costs on the standard basis –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (i) proportionately and reasonably incurred; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (ii) proportionate and reasonable in amount, or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (b) if it is assessing costs on the indemnity basis –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (i) unreasonably incurred; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (ii) unreasonable in amount. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (2) In particular, the court will give effect to any orders which have already been made. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (3) The court will also have regard to –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (a) the conduct of all the parties, including in particular –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (i) conduct before, as well as during, the proceedings; and

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (ii) the efforts made, if any, before and during the proceedings in order to try to resolve the dispute;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (b) the amount or value of any money or property involved;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (c) the importance of the matter to all the parties;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (d) the particular complexity of the matter or the difficulty or novelty of the questions raised;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (e) the skill, effort, specialised knowledge and responsibility involved;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (f) the time spent on the case;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (g) the place where and the circumstances in which work or any part of it was done; and

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (h) the receiving party’s last approved or agreed budget. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.4] (Rule 35.4(4) gives the court power to limit the amount that a party may recover with regard to the fees and expenses of an expert.)

## Amount of costs where costs are payable under a contract

## 44.5

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.5] (1) Subject to paragraphs (2) and (3), where the court assesses (whether by summary or detailed assessment) costs which are payable by the paying party to the receiving party under the terms of a contract, the costs payable under those terms are, unless the contract expressly provides otherwise, to be presumed to be costs which –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.5] (a) have been reasonably incurred; and

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.5] (b) are reasonable in amount,

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.5] and the court will assess them accordingly. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.5] (2) The presumptions in paragraph (1) are rebuttable. Practice Direction 44 – General rules about costs sets out circumstances where the court may order otherwise. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.5] (3) Paragraph (1) does not apply where the contract is between a solicitor and client. ## Procedure for assessing costs

## 44.6

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.6] (1) Where the court orders a party to pay costs to another party (other than fixed costs) it may –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.6] (a) make a summary assessment of the costs;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.6] (b) give directions for the summary assessment of the costs to be made at a later date; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.6] (c) order detailed assessment of the costs by a costs officer,

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.6] unless any rule, practice direction or other enactment provides otherwise. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.6] (Practice Direction 44 – General rules about costs sets out the factors which will affect the court’s decision under paragraph (1).)

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.6] (2) Where a direction has been given under paragraph (1)(b), another judge who could have decided the claim or application which gave rise to the costs order may make the summary assessment if there is good reason to do so. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.6] (3) A party may recover the fixed costs specified in Part 45 in accordance with that Part. ## Time for complying with an order for costs

## 44.7

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.7] (1) A party must comply with an order for the payment of costs within 14 days of –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.7] (a) the date of the judgment or order if it states the amount of those costs;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.7] (b) if the amount of those costs (or part of them) is decided later in accordance with Part 47, the date of the certificate which states the amount; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.7] (c) in either case, such other date as the court may specify. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.7] (Part 47 sets out the procedure for detailed assessment of costs.)

## Legal representative’s duty to notify the party

## 44.8 Where –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.8 Where –] (a) the court makes a costs order against a legally represented party; and

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.8 Where –] (b) the party is not present when the order is made,

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.8 Where –] the party’s legal representative must notify that party in writing of the costs order no later than 7 days after the legal representative receives notice of the order. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.8 Where –] (Paragraph 10.1 of Practice Direction 44 defines ‘party’ for the purposes of this rule.)

## Cases where costs orders deemed to have been made

## 44.9

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.9] (1) Subject to paragraph (2), where a right to costs arises under –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.9] (a) rule 3.7 or 3.7A1 (defendant’s right to costs where claim is struck out for non-payment of fees);

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.9] (a1) rule 3.7B (sanctions for dishonouring cheque);

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.9] (b) rule 36.13(1) or (2) (claimant’s entitlement to costs where a Part 36 offer is accepted); or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.9] (c) rule 38.6 (defendant’s right to costs where claimant discontinues),

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.9] a costs order will be deemed to have been made on the standard basis, save that where the claim is one to which the provisions of Part 27 or Part 45 would otherwise apply, the costs shall be determined in accordance with those Parts. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.9] (2) Paragraph 1(b) does not apply where a Part 36 offer is accepted before the commencement of proceedings. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.9] (3) Where such an order is deemed to be made in favour of a party with pro bono representation, that party may apply for an order under section 194(3) of the 2007 Act. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.9] (4) Interest payable under section 17 of the Judgments Act 1838 5 or section 74 of the County Courts Act 1984 6 on the costs deemed to have been ordered under paragraph (1) will begin to run from the date on which the event which gave rise to the entitlement to costs occurred. ## Where the court makes no order for costs

## 44.10

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.10] (1) Where the court makes an order which does not mention costs –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.10] (a) subject to paragraphs (2) and (3), the general rule is that no party is entitled –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.10] (i) to costs; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.10] (ii) to seek an order under section 194(3) of the 2007 Act,

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.10] in relation to that order; but

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.10] (b) this does not affect any entitlement of a party to recover costs out of a fund held by that party as trustee or personal representative, or under any lease, mortgage or other security. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.10] (2) Where the court makes –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.10] (a) an order granting permission to appeal;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.10] (b) an order granting permission to apply for judicial review; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.10] (c) any other order or direction sought by a party on an application without notice,

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.10] and its order does not mention costs, it will be deemed to include an order for applicant’s costs in the case. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.10] (3) Any party affected by a deemed order for costs under paragraph (2) may apply at any time to vary the order. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.10] (4) The court hearing an appeal may, unless it dismisses the appeal, make orders about the costs of the proceedings giving rise to the appeal as well as the costs of the appeal. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.10] (5) Subject to any order made by the transferring court, where proceedings are transferred from one court to another, the court to which they are transferred may deal with all the costs, including the costs before the transfer. ## Court’s powers in relation to misconduct

## 44.11

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.11] (1) The court may make an order under this rule where –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.11] (a) a party or that party’s legal representative, in connection with a summary or detailed assessment, fails to comply with a rule, practice direction or court order; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.11] (b) it appears to the court that the conduct of a party or that party’s legal representative, before or during the proceedings or in the assessment proceedings, was unreasonable or improper. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.11] (2) Where paragraph (1) applies, the court may –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.11] (a) disallow all or part of the costs which are being assessed; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.11] (b) order the party at fault or that party’s legal representative to pay costs which that party or legal representative has caused any other party to incur. [PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.11] (3) Where –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.11] (a) the court makes an order under paragraph (2) against a legally represented party; and

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.11] (b) the party is not present when the order is made,

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.11] the party’s legal representative must notify that party in writing of the order no later than 7 days after the legal representative receives notice of the order. ## Set Off

## 44.12

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.12] (1) Where a party entitled to costs is also liable to pay costs, the court may assess the costs which that party is liable to pay and either –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.12] (a) set off the amount assessed against the amount the party is entitled to be paid and direct that party to pay any balance; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.12] (b) delay the issue of a certificate for the costs to which the party is entitled until the party has paid the amount which that party is liable to pay

## II QUALIFIED ONE-WAY COSTS SHIFTING

## Qualified one-way costs shifting: scope and interpretation

## 44.13

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.13] (1) This Section applies to proceedings which include a claim for damages –

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.13] (a) for personal injuries;

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.13] (b) under the Fatal Accidents Act 1976 7 ; or

[PART 44 – GENERAL RULES ABOUT COSTS > 44.13] (c) which arises out of death or personal injury and survives for the benefit of an estate by virtue of section 1(1) of the Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1934 8 ,

